  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{\u2033}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
